1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:13,406
This is Königsberg, a city on the Pregel river, with four pieces of land and seven bridges.

2
00:00:13,406 --> 00:00:15,593
The people there liked to walk,

3
00:00:15,593 --> 00:00:21,450
and they wanted a route that crossed every bridge exactly once and came home again.

4
00:00:21,816 --> 00:00:28,224
Start on the left bank and cross to the island above, and from there go across to the right bank.

5
00:00:28,224 --> 00:00:34,704
Then walk down to the island below and back to the left bank, and two more bridges are behind us.

6
00:00:34,704 --> 00:00:41,950
Six of the seven bridges are behind us, and we are standing on the right bank with one bridge still waiting.

7
00:00:42,316 --> 00:00:49,904
Six bridges crossed and one still standing, so this route has failed, but maybe a cleverer route exists.

8
00:00:49,904 --> 00:00:57,670
In seventeen thirty six a mathematician named Leonhard Euler asked a sharper question about all routes at

9
00:00:57,670 --> 00:00:58,040
once.

10
00:00:58,916 --> 00:01:04,888
Now Euler threw away the river and the houses, and kept only what a walk can feel.

11
00:01:04,888 --> 00:01:08,073
Every piece of land becomes a single point,

12
00:01:08,073 --> 00:01:13,332
and every bridge becomes a line joining the two points that it touches.

13
00:01:13,683 --> 00:01:19,749
That leaves four points and seven lines, and the whole problem is now about these lines.

14
00:01:19,749 --> 00:01:25,163
Look at the island above and count the lines that touch it, and you will find five.

15
00:01:25,163 --> 00:01:33,071
The other three points each have three lines, so the four numbers are five, three, three and three.

16
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:40,238
The number of lines at a point is called its degree, and a point with no lines at all is isolated.

17
00:01:40,238 --> 00:01:47,386
Here no point is isolated, and every one of the four degrees is odd, which is the difficulty.

18
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:55,048
Euler turned the walk into a pen that traces every line once and comes back to where it started.

19
00:01:55,048 --> 00:01:59,368
The pen never lifts, and no line is traced twice.

20
00:01:59,368 --> 00:02:04,828
So think about one point of the picture and the visits the pen makes to it.

21
00:02:05,183 --> 00:02:10,623
Every visit to a point uses one line to arrive and another line to leave.

22
00:02:10,623 --> 00:02:15,859
That means the lines at the point can be paired up, one arrival with one departure,

23
00:02:15,859 --> 00:02:18,193
which forces their number to be even.

24
00:02:18,193 --> 00:02:26,089
Here the points have degrees three, five, three and three, and not one of those numbers is even.

25
00:02:26,450 --> 00:02:32,364
So at every point of Königsberg a walk would be left with one line that it cannot use,

26
00:02:32,364 --> 00:02:34,222
and the tour is impossible.

27
00:02:34,222 --> 00:02:41,222
In seventeen thirty six Euler proved it, and he also found exactly when a tour is possible.

28
00:02:42,083 --> 00:02:46,627
A walk that uses every line exactly once is called an Eulerian walk,

29
00:02:46,627 --> 00:02:50,637
and if it ends where it started we call it an Eulerian tour.

30
00:02:50,637 --> 00:02:56,373
Königsberg asked for such a tour, and now we want to know which graphs have one.

31
00:02:56,750 --> 00:03:02,252
Euler's theorem answers the question with two conditions that we can check by looking.

32
00:03:02,252 --> 00:03:09,064
A graph has an Eulerian tour exactly when every degree is even and the graph is connected.

33
00:03:09,064 --> 00:03:15,808
The only exception is a point with no lines at all, which a walk simply never visits.

34
00:03:16,183 --> 00:03:23,841
In this little graph every degree is even, and the whole graph is connected, so a tour must exist.

35
00:03:23,841 --> 00:03:30,669
The lonely point at the bottom left has no lines, so its degree is zero, and the walk ignores it.

36
00:03:30,669 --> 00:03:37,737
Here is one tour, and it walks around the left triangle, then the right one, and returns.

37
00:03:38,516 --> 00:03:45,630
So the same test decides both puzzles, the seven bridges and this little graph of two triangles.

38
00:03:45,630 --> 00:03:51,458
You count what touches each point, and you look at whether the picture holds together.

